  "oracle": "D3D8 public wrapper owns one D3D9 factory reference; public COM identity/refcount remain D3D8. Adapter count/monitor delegate through separate-SDK private ABI. Existing Windows8 SDK120/220 boundary retained. One core process attachment is borrowed by the frontend rather than attached twice. Reference requires only 0/3/0 compatibility Hellos and no backend-factory Hello,globally unique actual wire handles,three legacy8/ordinary9 devices,one D3D9 cached declaration,and one final session/owner end. Expected pixels are five independent constant colors.",
  "scope": "Stage4K2 migrates D3D8 factory creation/lifetime,adapter-count/monitor queries and module process ownership to private D3D9 core. Backend-root creation suppresses its own Hello so D3D8 still publishes only its compatibility profile. Core owns the single attachment;D3D8 borrows the canonical process and delegates final flush/release. Native69764 checks,512 SDK/owner profiles,16384 delegated rounds,12 assertion mutants,four old actual constructor/release/count/module controls,and two sanitizer families. XP128 factory cycles/264 identity checks,two SDK120/220 D3D8 device/module lifetimes with application root refs released while child device retains wrapper,ordinary D3D9 continues after each unload;three devices/one declaration,three Hellos 0/3/0,one owner/session,five UP/readbacks. Actual D3D8 device/resource implementations and remaining caps/PP/format/state/CopyRects/handle mapping still await migration. Full retail/Reset/loss/thread/format/MSAA/fullscreen/multibuffering,native HAL,games/FPS and overall stages4/5/6 open. Host/protocol/shader/bundle and DDraw unchanged.",
